Chapter: BvtRingNew
  cites Uoaix chapter CompositeEvents
  cites Uoaix chapter BvtCheck

 Every connection's pulse asks the event ring for what it has not heard; the ask reads the rows newer than heard, not
 all 1024, unless the row after heard does not hold its sequence (the ring grew under it), when it reads them all.
  bri-add : CeRing, Integer -> Integer
  bri-add (ring) (n) = if n == 0 then 0 else let a = ce-add ring 7 1000 1000 [28, 0, 5, 1, 2] in bri-add ring (n - 1)
  bvt-ring-new : Integer -> [Console] Integer
  bvt-ring-new (unused) = let ring = ce-new 0
    in let added = bri-add ring 1500
    in let fresh = ce-pending ring 9 (\s -> False) (\s -> False) True 1000 1000 1400 0 []
    in let stale = list-at ring.events (int-mod 1000 (list-length ring.events))
    in let forged = __record-set stale "sequence" 99999
    in let skipped = ce-pending ring 9 (\s -> False) (\s -> False) True 1000 1000 1400 0 []
    in let after = list-at ring.events (int-mod 1401 (list-length ring.events))
    in let moved = __record-set after "sequence" 0
    in let full = ce-pending ring 9 (\s -> False) (\s -> False) True 1000 1000 1400 0 []
    in act
      a <- bvt-check "a listener hears the 100 events past its last, in a wrapped ring" (list-length fresh == 100)
      b <- bvt-check "the ask reads only the rows newer than heard: an older row forged past heard is not read" (list-length skipped == 100)
      c <- bvt-check "a ring whose next row does not hold its sequence is read whole, so a grown ring loses nothing" (list-length full == 100)
      a + b + c
    end
